WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.850
Today, Dominic Toretto is one of Hollywood's
most beloved action heroes — a streetwise,

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:11.120
American family man whose uncanny abilities
behind the wheel have elevated him from truck-robbing

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.680
street racer to globe-trotting, world-saving
adventurer.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.010
Even still, like most heroes, he's had his
fair share of mistakes along the way.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:25.359
As a boy growing up in Los Angeles, Dominic
Toretto idolizes his father, a local stock

00:00:25.359 --> 00:00:26.519
car racer.

00:00:26.519 --> 00:00:30.819
Dom's father models many of the values that
our hero comes to represent later in life

00:00:30.819 --> 00:00:36.430
— he loves cars and driving, he's a man
of faith and a community leader, and nothing

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:38.380
is more important to him than his family.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:44.460
So, when Dom witnesses his father's death
on the racetrack, he is absolutely devastated.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:48.450
Dom's father is killed in an accident caused
by a fellow racer named Kenny Linder, who

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:53.800
clips the elder Toretto's bumper and sends
him crashing into a wall at terminal speeds.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:59.530
Dom runs into Linder the week after the accident
and, enraged by what he feels was a preventable

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:01.880
accident, attacks Linder with a wrench.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:06.340
Dom only plans on hitting him once but then
loses control of himself and leaves Linder

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.180
seriously injured.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:12.740
Dom not only ends Linder's racing career,
but he leaves him physically unable to drive

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.170
for years to come.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:17.970
Dom is sentenced to two years in prison for
the assault, and he's banned from professional

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.540
racing for life.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:24.100
Dom's assault of Kenny Linder is the inciting
incident that sets the entire "Fast Saga"

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:25.100
into motion.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:28.920
Dominic Toretto may have had a career as a
legitimate stock car racer and wouldn't have

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.900
had to turn to street racing to satisfy his
need for speed or steal electronics to finance

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:34.900
his sister's education.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:40.060
Early in his criminal career, Dom has a business
arrangement with Johnny Tran, a fellow thief

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:41.540
and street racer.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:44.820
Johnny is a dangerous man with a vicious streak.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:48.340
Dom would be wise to stay on his good side,
but he risks their business relationship when

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:52.280
he sleeps with Johnny's sister in an apparently
casual fling.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:56.470
Johnny takes offense to this, and he and Dom
part, carving up their territory and agreeing

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:58.300
to stay out of each other's way.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.850
“Time for you to go do your own thing.”

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:05.220
But in "The Fast and the Furious," Dom accidentally
violates that truce when he and Brian O'Conner

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:08.560
ride through Johnny's turf, reigniting their
rivalry.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:13.070
Brian doesn't help matters either, as he suspects
Johnny of coordinating a string of truck robberies

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:17.560
and arranges a police raid of his home, humiliating
him in front of his family.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:21.310
Johnny mistakenly believes that Dom is the
one who ratted him out, and he confronts him

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:24.819
at the “Race Wars” competition in front
of their peers on the racing scene.

00:02:24.819 --> 00:02:30.160
To make matters worse, Dom's friend, Jesse,
challenges Johnny to a race and loses, then

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.010
flees out of panic rather than handing over
his pink slip as agreed.

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:38.060
Johnny retaliates with a drive-by shooting
of the Toretto family home in which Jesse

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:42.330
is killed, forcing Brian to return fire and
kill Johnny.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:47.220
Dom isn't solely to blame for all this, but
had he not put his libido ahead of his business,

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:50.450
the bloodshed between the Tran and Toretto
crews might've been avoided.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:57.840
In "The Fast and the Furious," Dom makes one
major, fateful mistake that profoundly reshapes

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.810
the rest of his life when he welcomes a relative
stranger, street racer and mechanic "Brian

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:05.350
Earl Spilner," into his inner circle.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:09.930
Dom is initially suspicious and dismissive
of Brian, who — in addition to poking his

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:14.510
head into Dom's domain as a street racer — also
frequents the Toretto family grocery to flirt

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:16.120
with Dom's sister, Mia.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:20.110
But after Brian helps Dom avoid arrest for
street racing, Dom begins to warm up to this

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:24.810
guy and gradually lets him into his life and
closer to his criminal enterprise.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:30.410
Unbeknownst to Dom, Brian Spilner is actually
Brian O'Conner, an undercover LAPD officer

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:35.290
assigned to investigate a series of truck
robberies for which Dom and his crew are responsible.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:40.380
Dom ignores the signs of Brian's true agenda
and even the warnings of his close friend,

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:44.300
Vince, until it's too late and Dom's operation
is exposed.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:48.220
Brian allows Dom to escape from the police,
but Dom is still forced to leave the country

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:52.769
and embark on a series of increasingly daring
heists to make a living until his eventual

00:03:52.769 --> 00:03:55.120
pardon in "Fast &amp; Furious 6."

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:59.650
While trusting Brian O'Conner is certainly
a mistake in the moment, eventually Brian

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:03.050
and Dom reconcile and come to see each other
as brothers.

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:07.849
Brian and Mia's relationship also stands the
test of time, and Dom becomes the proud uncle

00:04:07.849 --> 00:04:08.849
to their two children.

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:14.610
The original "Fast and the Furious" introduces
a large-scale racing event and car show held

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:19.229
in the desert outside of Los Angeles where
street racers can compete for glory, cash,

00:04:19.229 --> 00:04:22.019
and pink slips without interference from the
police.

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:27.759
It's a serious, well-organized enterprise
complete with DJs, dancers, and a security

00:04:27.759 --> 00:04:28.759
staff.

00:04:28.759 --> 00:04:32.960
In "Furious 7," Dom reveals that he and Letty
Ortiz invented the ritual, which means that

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:39.490
Dom and Letty may also be responsible for
its very unfortunate name: Race Wars.

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:43.389
Dom and Letty would've been pretty young when
they came up with this name and likely didn't

00:04:43.389 --> 00:04:48.339
give much thought to the other connotations
of the phrase "race war," but now we all have

00:04:48.339 --> 00:04:51.340
to live with it, and we don't just mean in
the movies.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:55.919
The Race Wars in "The Fast and the Furious"
has inspired similar, regularly recurring

00:04:55.919 --> 00:05:00.509
events around the world, including in the
United States, Germany, and Australia.

00:05:00.509 --> 00:05:03.680
The idea of creating a space where street
racers can show off their skills and their

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:07.830
machines in style and in relative safety is
a novel one.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:12.050
The problem is that these real-life events
also borrow their name from the "Fast and

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:17.639
Furious" films, going with the similar but
legally distinct one word title of "Racewars."

00:05:17.639 --> 00:05:18.809
Thanks for that one, Dom!

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:23.849
In "Fast &amp; Furious" and its prequel short
film, "Los Bandoleros," Dom is revealed to

00:05:23.849 --> 00:05:27.900
be on the run from authorities and living
in the Dominican Republic, where he and his

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:30.969
new crew plan to steal a tanker of gasoline.

00:05:30.969 --> 00:05:35.240
On the eve of the heist, Dom's girlfriend
from Los Angeles, Letty Ortiz, arrives in

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:36.659
town looking for him.

00:05:36.659 --> 00:05:40.990
The couple rekindles their relationship and,
as revealed in "Furious 7," are married in

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:42.370
a private ceremony.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:46.270
The heist is a success, but law enforcement
is catching up to them.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:50.610
Dom fears that his capture may be inevitable,
so he slips away while Letty is asleep, hoping

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:54.139
that leaving her behind might save her from
going down with him.

00:05:54.139 --> 00:05:56.800
This turns out to be a grave error.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:01.439
Still hoping for her happy ending, Letty returns
to the United States and offers to work undercover

00:06:01.439 --> 00:06:06.580
for the FBI in exchange for a pardon for Dom
so that the two of them can both go home.

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:11.449
When Letty's cover is blown, she's seriously
wounded and presumed killed.

00:06:11.449 --> 00:06:16.279
Now an amnesiac, Letty then joins up with
the mercenary crew led by Owen Shaw and goes

00:06:16.279 --> 00:06:18.770
head-to-head against Dom and his team.

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:23.129
While she ultimately rejoins Dom's family
late in "Fast &amp; Furious 6," Letty doesn't

00:06:23.129 --> 00:06:25.949
regain her memories until the end of "Furious
7."

00:06:25.949 --> 00:06:30.340
Had Dom respected Letty's choice to share
the risk of their outlaw lifestyle, both of

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:32.539
them could have been spared a lot of heartache.

00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:37.899
In "Fast &amp; Furious," Dom goes hunting for
Arturo Braga, the international drug dealer

00:06:37.899 --> 00:06:40.509
he believes is responsible for Letty's death.

00:06:40.509 --> 00:06:44.360
In order to get close to Braga, Dom auditions
to join the fleet of performance drivers that

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:47.939
Braga uses to smuggle his product across the
Mexican-U.S. border.

00:06:47.939 --> 00:06:51.499
Once he's admitted into their ranks, Dom begins
working closely with a man calling himself

00:06:51.499 --> 00:06:54.990
Ramon Campos who claims to be an associate
of Braga's.

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:59.629
This turns out to be a ruse, as Campos is
actually Braga himself, working under an assumed

00:06:59.629 --> 00:07:03.340
identity in order to keep the upper hand in
situations like this one.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:07.529
To throw people off his scent, Braga employs
a decoy to portray him in public.

00:07:07.529 --> 00:07:12.659
This ploy works just long enough for Braga
to escape the trap set for him by Dom, Brian,

00:07:12.659 --> 00:07:16.050
and the FBI, allowing him to escape to Mexico.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:20.680
Just as in "The Fast &amp; the Furious," Dom spends
most of this film missing something that's

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:22.969
right under his nose.

00:07:22.969 --> 00:07:27.289
Dom and Brian capture Braga in Mexico soon
after, but Braga should be grateful that Dom

00:07:27.289 --> 00:07:29.360
didn't determine his true identity earlier.

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:34.319
If Dom had known who he was when they first
met, he probably would've killed him on the

00:07:34.319 --> 00:07:35.319
spot.

00:07:36.319 --> 00:07:40.389
In "Fast Five," Dom, Brian, and Mia find themselves
in Rio de Janeiro, where their old friend

00:07:40.389 --> 00:07:45.030
Vince offers them each a spot on a team that
plans to hijack three performance cars from

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:46.800
aboard a moving train.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:50.589
Vince says that the goal of the heist is to
sell the cars quickly for cash on the black

00:07:50.589 --> 00:07:53.699
market, but he hides their true, secret objective.

00:07:53.699 --> 00:07:57.050
Vince also chooses not to reveal that the
cars they're stealing have been repossessed

00:07:57.050 --> 00:08:02.339
by the United States Drug Enforcement Agency
and that there will be armed DEA agents on

00:08:02.339 --> 00:08:03.339
the train.

00:08:03.339 --> 00:08:06.889
When the heist begins, Dom quickly senses
that the other members of their crew have

00:08:06.889 --> 00:08:10.099
information that he, Brian, and Mia do not.

00:08:10.099 --> 00:08:14.369
He calls an audible, telling Mia to take the
car that seems most important and hide it

00:08:14.369 --> 00:08:17.550
away rather than drive it to the agreed-upon
rendezvous point.

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:19.050
“Change of Plans.

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:21.039
Wait for my call.”

00:08:21.039 --> 00:08:25.229
This car turns out to be the real target of
the heist, as it contains a computer chip

00:08:25.229 --> 00:08:28.159
belonging to local drug baron Hernan Reyes.

00:08:28.159 --> 00:08:33.370
The mission soon barrels out of control, and
three of Reyes' men and three DEA agents are

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:34.370
killed.

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:39.580
This puts Dom, Brian, and Mia in the crosshairs
of both Reyes and the U.S. government.

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:43.680
As usual, the Torettos find a way to turn
this situation to their advantage.

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:48.910
Even still, the entire mess, including six
fatalities, might've been avoided if Dom had

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:51.310
just allowed the heist to play out as planned.

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:57.150
In "Fast &amp; Furious 6," Dom gets the news that
the love of his life, Letty Ortiz, is still

00:08:57.150 --> 00:09:00.820
alive and a member of Owen Shaw's hardened
mercenary crew.

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:05.650
At the behest of DSS Agent Luke Hobbs, Dom
assembles his team in the hopes of reuniting

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:10.270
with Letty and bringing her home, as well
as stopping Shaw from assembling the components

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:12.750
of a dangerous electromagnetic weapon.

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:16.680
When Dom catches up to Letty in the aftermath
of a high-speed chase in London, he approaches

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:18.030
her with his guard down.

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:22.740
Unbeknownst to Dom, Letty has no memory of
Dom or their life together, and she shoots

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:25.570
him in the shoulder, seemingly without a thought.

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:29.280
Dom's mistake here is understandable, as it
would've been difficult for him to predict

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:33.610
that someone he's known since childhood and
is secretly married to would've completely

00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:34.770
forgotten him.

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:39.150
But any error that results in getting shot
is still a pretty big deal.

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:43.060
Also, according to Letty herself, it could've
been a lot worse; Letty later claims that

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:46.130
her aim was off and that she'd planned to
shoot to kill.

00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:50.460
Dom disputes this, but assuming she's telling
the truth, Dom's mistake might well have cost

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:51.540
him his life.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:56.140
Thankfully, this incident doesn't discourage
Dom from reaching out to Letty, and his implicit

00:09:56.140 --> 00:10:00.390
trust in her is part of what convinces Letty
to switch sides and help to take Shaw down.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:06.200
"Furious 7" pits Dom against Deckard Shaw,
who's out for revenge after the defeat of

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:07.280
his brother, Owen.

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:12.240
The villain proves he means business by killing
Dom's friend, Han, and destroying the Toretto's

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:13.520
beloved family home.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:18.270
Next, Shaw stalks the crew at Han's funeral,
and Dom makes the questionable decision of

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:22.060
chasing after him alone rather than taking
the rest of the crew with him.

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:26.120
This stand-off ends with the two of them in
their cars playing chicken, and neither of

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:27.120
them blink.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:31.320
Dom charges Shaw head-on with his Plymouth
Road Runner, unaware that Shaw has heavily

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:34.120
reinforced the chassis of his Maserati Ghibli.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:38.620
Dom steps out of his wrecked Plymouth as the
more injured of the two men and derides Shaw

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:41.250
for what he sees as an underhanded tactic.

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:45.070
But Dom hasn't caught on that he's no longer
living in the world of street racing or even

00:10:45.070 --> 00:10:46.220
street-level crime.

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:51.480
He's in Shaw's world now — a world of espionage
in which Dom can't expect a sense of fair

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:52.480
play.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:56.430
If not for the timely arrival of Mr. Nobody
and his strike force, Shaw would've shot Dom

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:57.960
dead right then and there.

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:00.800
By the end of "Furious 7," Dom has learned
his lesson.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:04.790
When Dom and Shaw play chicken again in the
film's climax, Dom pulls a wheelie and jumps

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:08.630
the front of his signature Charger over the
top of Shaw's Aston Martin, giving him the

00:11:08.630 --> 00:11:10.500
upper hand in their final street fight.

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:16.050
In "The Fate of the Furious," Dom is extorted
into doing the bidding of international cyber

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:20.330
terrorist Cipher, who hopes to use his skills
to help spark a nuclear war.

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:25.800
Cipher lures Dom into her employ by capturing
his ex-girlfriend, Elena Neves, along with

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:29.750
Marcos, the infant son Dom didn't know he
had.

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:34.490
With the two of them held hostage, Cipher
has total leverage over Dom, which she uses

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:39.720
to compel him to betray his family, steal
nuclear launch codes, and help hijack a Russian

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:40.720
submarine.

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:45.220
As awful as this situation is, it's difficult
to imagine what Dom could've done differently

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:46.430
to avoid it.

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:51.370
Dom begins planning for his escape and Elena
and Marcos' rescue as soon as he can, secretly

00:11:51.370 --> 00:11:56.740
contacting the Shaw family to track and commandeer
Cipher's flying base of operations.

00:11:56.740 --> 00:12:01.790
Still, he surely wishes he'd been able to
escape from Cipher's clutches earlier.

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:06.000
During Dom's mission for Cipher in New York,
Dom prevents her lieutenant, Rhodes, from

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:10.120
killing Letty, and Cipher punishes Dom by
having Elena executed.

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:14.850
Dom isn't responsible for Elena's death, and
he obviously couldn't allow Letty to die either,

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:19.120
but he'll still have to live with the regret
over essentially choosing one of them to be

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:20.120
killed.

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:23.610
Thankfully, Dom is at least able to free himself
from Cipher in time to fulfill his promise

00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:26.360
to Elena that he would keep their son safe.

